        prov:wasAssociatedWith <http://example.org/node/orchestrator> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk1> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Public Tender - Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) Implementation of an AI assistance solution for hospital staff 1. Context and objectives The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) is a public regional hospital serving the Belval region (approximately 1,200 beds and 4,500 staff across medical, care and medico-administrative functions). As part of The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) digital transformation and of the national Ségur du numérique en santé programme, The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) wishes to improve the efficiency of The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) staff and the quality and safety of the care delivered to patients. Staff face a growing complexity of procedures, a multiplicity of information sources (care protocols, recommendations from the Haute Autorité de Santé (HAS), internal procedures, regulatory texts, business systems) and an increasing volume of administrative and documentation work that reduces the time available for patient care. In this context, The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) wishes to procure, deploy and operate an artificial intelligence solution that assists staff in staff daily activities - facilitating access to information, improving the quality of written outputs, and reducing processing and documentation times - while remaining strictly a non-decisional support tool. The present contract covers the design, implementation and deployment of such a solution. 2. General description of the need The expected solution must provide hospital staff with an intelligent assistance tool, accessible from hospital staff work environment, capable of supporting hospital staff in hospital staff clinical-administrative activities. an intelligent assistance tool, accessible from their work environment, capable of supporting them in their clinical-administrative activities may notably cover: retrieval of information from internal procedures, care protocols and applicable regulation; assistance in drafting medical and administrative documents; support in handling dossiers (admissions, coding, medico-social pathways, etc.); support for the continuity of care between professionals, shifts and services. The solution must fit into the existing practices of staff and must not introduce a rupture in clinical or administrative workflows. 3. Functional scope 3.1 Information consultation assistance. The solution must let staff quickly access reliable, up-to-date information drawn from the authority's internal procedures, care protocols and HAS recommendations, applicable regulatory texts, and internal documents (guides, notes, reference frameworks, drug references). The solution must contextualise The solution answers to the staff member's request. 3.2 Drafting assistance. The solution must assist the drafting of clinical and administrative content, notably discharge summaries and medical reports (comptes-rendus), medical correspondence and patient letters, administrative documents and dossier syntheses. Generated content must be adapted to the clinical/administrative context and conform to expected quality standards. 3.3 Dossier-handling assistance. The solution must assist staff in analysing and handling dossiers by surfacing relevant information, recalling applicable rules, and suggesting actions or verifications. The solution must support medical coding (PMSI / ICD-10) for activity reporting (T2A). The solution must take the dossier context into account where available. No decision may be automated. 3.4 Collaboration and continuity of care. The solution must facilitate the continuity of care between professionals and services by enabling relevant information sharing, an understanding of the context of an in-progress patient pathway, and an efficient resumption of handling by another professional. 4. Regulatory and legal constraints 4.1 Personal and health data protection. The titleholder must guarantee strict compliance with the GDPR. Health data is processed as special-category data (GDPR Art. 9) and medical confidentiality (secret médical) must be preserved. Data must be hosted exclusively within the European Union; no transfer of data outside the European Union is authorised; sensitive data must be appropriately protected (encryption, pseudonymisation where relevant). 4.2 Health-data hosting (HDS). The infrastructure hosting The solution must hold the French Hébergeur de Données de Santé (HDS) certification. 4.3 Explainability and traceability. Recommendations or suggestions produced by The solution must be explainable. It must be possible, a posteriori, to understand the elements that led to an output (source grounding, audit trail), consistent with HAS guidance on the use of AI in healthcare. 4.4 Medical responsibility. The solution must be designed as an assistance tool. No clinical or medico-administrative decision may be automated without explicit validation by a qualified professional. The solution must be positioned so as not to require medical-device qualification (CE marking / MDR); should any use case approach medical-device qualification (CE marking / MDR), the bidder must explicitly address the implications. 5. Technical constraints 5.1 Performance. The solution must provide response times compatible with daily clinical use. As an indication, a response time below 2 seconds is expected for common queries. A high level of answer relevance is expected. 5.2 Availability. Continuity of care requires a high level of availability, including outside office hours (24/7 services). An availability target of the order of 99.9% is expected. 5.3 Integration and interoperability. The solution must integrate with the existing hospital information system (SIH) and the electronic patient record (DPI). The solution must support health interoperability standards (HL7 / FHIR), the national health identifier (INS), and the national framework (Ségur du numérique en santé, Mon espace santé / DMP). API-type interfaces are expected, as is integration with user directories. 6. Sovereignty constraints the authority's attaches particular importance to data sovereignty. Hosting must be performed on SecNumCloud-qualified infrastructure or equivalent, and particular attention will be paid to dependency on non-European suppliers. 7. Budget constraints The global budget allocated to the project is estimated at 900,000 over 5 years. The annual operating cost must remain controlled and compatible with the authority's budgetary capacity. The titleholder must propose an approach that optimises costs while guaranteeing the expected quality of service. 8. Environmental constraints As part of the authority's sustainable-development policy, the authority's wishes to limit the environmental footprint of the authority's information systems. The titleholder must propose a solution that contributes to reducing energy consumption and relies, as far as possible, on low-carbon infrastructure. 9. Deployment terms the project must be conducted progressively, with a pilot phase on a limited perimeter (one or two departments) followed by progressive generalisation to all services. Change-management support and staff training are expected. 10. Evaluation criteria Offers will be evaluated against the following criteria: quality and relevance of the proposed solution; compliance with regulatory requirements; level of security; data sovereignty; quality of integration with the existing SIH/DPI; total cost of the proposed solution; consideration of environmental issues; capacity for innovation." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message0> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk10> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "The client wishes to limit the environmental footprint of The client information systems, with a focus on reducing energy consumption and relying on low-carbon infrastructure. What measures will we implement to ensure that we proposed solution contributes to these sustainability goals, including the use of green hosting partners, digital sobriety practices, and carbon footprint reduction strategies? To address The client environmental concerns, we can leverage we existing capabilities as a CSR & Sustainability officer at we. we have established partnerships with green hosting providers, such as OuraneCloud in the EU region, which offers sovereign hosting, renewable energy, and a power usage effectiveness (PUE) of 1.2, as well as heat reuse. This alignment with low-carbon infrastructure will contribute to reducing the environmental footprint of our proposed solution. Furthermore, we implement digital sobriety practices, including model right-sizing, caching, and batching, which help minimize energy consumption and optimize resource utilization. we carbon reporting capability also enables we to monitor and assess the carbon footprint of we operations, ensuring transparency and facilitating continuous improvement. Additionally, we has a published trajectory to reduce carbon intensity per managed assistant year on year, demonstrating we commitment to ongoing sustainability efforts. By deploying our proposed solution on green hosting infrastructure and incorporating digital sobriety practices, we can effectively support The client goals of reducing energy consumption and relying on low-carbon infrastructure, thus contributing to a more sustainable information system." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message16> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk11> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Faber IA proposes a comprehensive solution to implement an artificial intelligence (AI) assistance tool for hospital staff at Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB). Faber IA project plan, based on Faber IA Hybrid delivery model, combines agile iterations with a phase-gate structure, ensuring a structured approach to delivering the project. The five phases - Framing, Design, Pilot, Generalisation, and Run - will be completed over approximately 20 months, with a total budget within the client's estimated global budget of €900,000 over five years. Faber IA solution, Atelier, is designed as an intelligent assistance tool that supports hospital staff in hospital staff daily activities. Our solution, Atelier provides retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) capabilities, leveraging natural language processing (NLP) and machine learning algorithms to offer explainable recommendations and suggestions. Our solution, Atelier assists with information retrieval from internal procedures, care protocols, and applicable regulations, drafting of medical and administrative documents, and handling dossiers. Our solution, Atelier also facilitates continuity of care between professionals, shifts, and services. To ensure strict compliance with GDPR, Faber IA implement measures such as encryption in transit using TLS 1.3 and encryption at rest using AES-256, pseudonymisation, and data minimisation techniques to protect sensitive data. Faber IA key management system is based on EU-based infrastructure, and Faber IA have a Data Protection Officer (DPO) appointed to oversee data protection activities. Faber IA also maintain audit logs of access and system outputs. Faber IA hosting partner, OuraneCloud, holds the HDS certification, ensuring that health data is hosted exclusively within EU and in compliance with French regulations. Faber IA do not transfer data outside EU, adhering to the client's requirement for data sovereignty. The infrastructure hosting Faber IA solution meets the requirements for SecNumCloud qualification and HDS certification. The proposed architecture involves deploying Our solution, Atelier on the Sovereign HDS tier of Our hosting partner, OuraneCloud, which meets all requirements related to hosting, performance, integration, and interoperability standards, while ensuring data sovereignty and compliance with regulatory constraints. deploying allows for horizontal scaling to ensure high throughput and maintain the required availability and response times. our solution provides response times below 2 seconds for common queries, with a median latency of 1.3 seconds and p95 query time below 2 seconds. The availability target is 99.9%, ensuring continuity of care. Faber IA also support health interoperability standards such as HL7/FHIR, INS, and the national framework (Ségur du numérique en santé, Mon espace santé/DMP). To address environmental concerns, Faber IA leverage Faber IA existing capabilities as a CSR & Sustainability officer at Faber IA. our solution is deployed on green hosting infrastructure, with a power usage effectiveness (PUE) of 1.2 and heat reuse. Faber IA implement digital sobriety practices, including model right-sizing, caching, and batching, to minimize energy consumption and optimize resource utilization. Faber IA budget proposal is within the client's estimated global budget of €900,000 over five years. Faber IA aim to maintain a target gross margin between 25% and 35%, with standard daily rates ranging from €700 to €1,300 per day, excluding VAT. Faber IA financial role works closely with the architect to keep the proposed configuration within the authority's budget envelope while preserving an acceptable margin. In conclusion, Our budget proposal addresses all requirements and constraints outlined in the call for tenders. Our solution, Atelier, provides an intelligent assistance tool that supports hospital staff in hospital staff daily activities, ensures strict compliance with GDPR, and meets technical, regulatory, and environmental requirements. Our are confident that Our team has the necessary expertise and capacity to deliver this project within the proposed timeline and budget." ;
        prov:wasDerivedFrom <http://example.org/node/message17> .

    <http://example.org/node/chunk2> a prov:Entity ;
        ex:hasText "Public Tender - Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) Implementation of an AI assistance solution for hospital staff 1. Context and objectives The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) is a public regional hospital serving the Belval region (approximately 1,200 beds and 4,500 staff across medical, care and medico-administrative functions). As part of The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) digital transformation and of the national Ségur du numérique en santé programme, The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) wishes to improve the efficiency of The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) staff and the quality and safety of the care delivered to patients. Staff face a growing complexity of procedures, a multiplicity of information sources (care protocols, recommendations from the Haute Autorité de Santé (HAS), internal procedures, regulatory texts, business systems) and an increasing volume of administrative and documentation work that reduces the time available for patient care. In this context, The Centre Hospitalier Régional de Belval (CHRB) wishes to procure, deploy and operate an artificial intelligence solution that assists staff in staff daily activities - facilitating access to information, improving the quality of written outputs, and reducing processing and documentation times - while remaining strictly a non-decisional support tool. The present contract covers the design, implementation and deployment of such a solution. 2. General description of the need The expected solution must provide hospital staff with an intelligent assistance tool, accessible from hospital staff work environment, capable of supporting hospital staff in hospital staff clinical-administrative activities. an intelligent assistance tool, accessible from their work environment, capable of supporting them in their clinical-administrative activities may notably cover: retrieval of information from internal procedures, care protocol